WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.884
A payment screen shows a payment, its refunds and its merchant.

2
00:00:06.884 --> 00:00:10.670
In MongoDB one record, a document, can hold a whole tree.

3
00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:14.389
So what goes inside the payment, and what stays outside?

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.511
Version one copies the tables.

5
00:00:16.511 --> 00:00:20.587
Refunds get their own collection, so the screen needs two reads.

6
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:26.421
Worse, two refunds of 6,000 arrive at once for this payment of 6,837.

7
00:00:26.421 --> 00:00:30.763
Each one checks the refunds, finds none, and inserts.

8
00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:32.648
Now 12,000 is refunded.

9
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.289
Stopping that takes a transaction across two documents.

10
00:00:36.289 --> 00:00:40.920
Version two puts the refunds inside the payment, next to a running total.

11
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.989
One read returns everything.

12
00:00:42.989 --> 00:00:53.039
And a write to one document is atomic: one update checks that the total plus 6,000 stays within the amount, pushes the refund, and adds to the total.

13
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.035
Two refunds race again.

14
00:00:55.035 --> 00:00:57.428
The first matches and applies.

15
00:00:57.428 --> 00:01:03.332
The second's check now fails, 12,000 is over 6,837, so it changes nothing.

16
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:05.227
The merchant is different.

17
00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:10.308
Thousands of payments share it, so the payment holds only its id: a reference.

18
00:01:10.308 --> 00:01:13.175
A new address is one write, to one document.

19
00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:17.619
The price is a second read whenever the screen shows the merchant's name.

20
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:21.118
Embedding goes wrong when an array has no end.

21
00:01:21.118 --> 00:01:25.975
Put every payment inside its merchant, and M-0042 adds 40,000 a day.

22
00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:30.453
In under three days the document hits MongoDB's 16 MB limit.

23
00:01:30.453 --> 00:01:35.853
Long before that, every read of the merchant drags the whole history along.

24
00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:40.488
So reference what grows without limit: payments stay their own documents.

25
00:01:40.488 --> 00:01:44.516
If a screen needs recent payments, keep a summary capped at three.

26
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.301
Embed what you read and change together, and what stays small.

27
00:01:48.301 --> 00:01:51.263
Reference what's shared, or what grows without end.
